ction/e4edb4b9e6e30eb97eb427f1ae3516b4961fc77766c70c53a5612b2219f68c8c" "q-107654/selection/b758cee6961d06b43238338c2e3afe67eba24fef885f0e8caf7d1487172c4d1e" "q-107654/selection/b6cb96f41ad4c52d67af4e5dfd289f7a3b436e2b40caf58ddaa41cd440729840"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-107654" :question/id #uuid "eb06b6e8-09c0-5357-8229-6e5ebd9caeb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-107654" :question/problem "Express $S=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{5}{4}+\\frac{5}{6}+⋯+\\frac{5}{26}+\\frac{5}{28}$ in sigma notation." :question/worked-solution "We first examine the numerators and denominators of the corresponding sequence.\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2},\\,\\frac{5}{4},\\,\\frac{5}{6},\\,…,\\,\\frac{5}{26},\\,\\frac{5}{28}\n$$\n\nOur goal is to find a pattern in the terms. To do this, we note the following:\n\n- The numerators form the following sequence:\n\n$$\n5,\\,5,\\,5,\\,…,\\,5,\\,5\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of this sequence is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=5.\n$$\n\n- The denominators form the following sequence:\n\n$$\n2,\\,4,\\,…,\\,26,\\,28\n$$\n\nThese values are multiples of $2,$ and since $28÷2=14,$ there are $14$ terms in total. The $n$th term of this sequence is given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=2n,\\,1\\le n\\le 14.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $n$th term of the sequence\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2},\\,\\frac{5}{4},\\,\\frac{5}{6},\\,…,\\,\\frac{5}{26},\\,\\frac{5}{28}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{b}_{n}}=\\frac{5}{2n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ ranges from $n=1$ to $n=14.$ \n\nTherefore, we can express our series using sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S={\\sum }_{n=1}^{14}\\frac{5}{2n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-107654/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "50fb047f-8c8d-56a6-9093-29a68cd89a90"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-107654"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-107696/selection/0bb88a3df30b58f32a24ffc7554f3e5112ec8f39bb9bfa5304f01d1067e1945e" :answer/id #